<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Web apps that work offline need to show which of their controls still work. Today that takes script that
listens for <code>online</code> and <code>offline</code> events and toggles a class.

<div class=example id=example-offline>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (network: offline) {
      .needs-network { opacity: 0.5; pointer-events: none; }
      .offline-banner { display: block; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

Pages also download more than they need to on connections that cost the user money. Operating systems know
when a connection is metered: cellular data, a phone's hotspot, a network the user has marked as metered,
or Low Data Mode. '@media/prefers-reduced-data' covers users who have asked for less data; many users on a
metered connection haven't, and still pay for every megabyte.

<div class=example id=example-metered>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (connection: metered), (prefers-reduced-data: reduce) {
      .hero video { display: none; }
      .hero { background-image: url(hero-still.jpg); }
    }
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;picture>
      &lt;source media="(connection: metered)" srcset="photo-800.jpg">
      &lt;img src="photo-2400.jpg" alt="">
    &lt;/picture>
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=network-feature>The '@media/network' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: network
Value: online | offline
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/network' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. In a [=document=] |d|:

: <dfn value for=@media/network>online</dfn>
:: <code>navigator.onLine</code> would return true in |d|'s relevant global object.
: <dfn value for=@media/network>offline</dfn>
:: <code>navigator.onLine</code> would return false. This value evaluates to false in a
    [=boolean context=], so <code>(network)</code> means online.

The value changes when, and only when, the user agent fires the <code>online</code> or
<code>offline</code> event at |d|'s relevant global object, in the same task.

<div class=note>
    The feature has the same value, at the same moments, as <code>navigator.onLine</code>, which every
    browser supports in every document, including cross-origin frames. It has the same weakness: "online"
    means the user agent believes it can reach the network, which can be true on a network with no route
    to the internet.
</div>


<h2 id=connection-feature>The '@media/connection' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: connection
Value: unmetered | metered
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/connection' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. Its [=actual value=] is:

: <dfn value for=@media/connection>metered</dfn>
:: The operating system reports that the network the user agent uses for the document's requests is
    metered or constrained: it has a cost per amount of data, a data limit, or a mode that asks apps to
    use less data.
: <dfn value for=@media/connection>unmetered</dfn>
:: Otherwise, including when the user agent doesn't know, or the user agent is offline.

<div class=note>
    For example: Android reports a network without the "not metered" capability; Apple platforms report a
    path as expensive, which includes cellular and personal hotspots, or as constrained, which is Low
    Data Mode; Windows reports a connection cost other than unrestricted, which includes networks the user
    has set as metered.
</div>

The user agent must derive the value only from these reports of the operating system, and from a setting
in the user agent that marks the current network as metered. It must not derive it from the type of
network, such as cellular, except where the operating system itself treats that type as metered, and must
not derive it from the network's speed, name, or address.

The user agent must not reveal anything else about the network through this feature.

<h3 id=connection-changes>Changes</h3>

The '@media/connection' feature uses [=delayed change delivery=] [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]], with a
[=maximum delay=] of 60 seconds and a [=minimum interval=] of 10 minutes.

<div class=note>
    A page that notices a minute late that the user has left their Wi-Fi uses a little more data, once.
    [[#privacy-correlation]] explains why the delay is there.
</div>

<h3 id=connection-reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

The '@media/connection' feature is [=under user control=]. In a [=reduced-exposure context=], it is
''connection/unmetered''.

<div class=note>
    The '@media/network' feature has no reduced exposure: it reveals nothing that
    <code>navigator.onLine</code> doesn't, and offline apps need it to work.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-network>The '@media/network' feature</h3>

The '@media/network' feature reveals exactly what <code>navigator.onLine</code> and the
<code>online</code> and <code>offline</code> events reveal, to the same documents, at the same moments.
It adds no information to script, and no timing.

Without script, a style sheet can't report ''network/offline'' when it happens, because the user agent
can't send the request. When the user agent comes back online, a rule under ''network/online'' can load a
resource, which tells the server when the connection returned. Any request the page makes after reconnecting
tells the server the same thing.

A user is online when a page loads, so the feature adds no fingerprinting information.

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What '@media/connection' reveals</h3>

Directly, the feature reveals whether the operating system considers the current network metered. Less
obviously, it can reveal:

* **Whether the user is on cellular data.** Phones treat cellular data as metered and most Wi-Fi as not,
    so on a phone the value is close to "cellular or Wi-Fi".
* **Where the user is.** A change from ''connection/unmetered'' to ''connection/metered'' usually means
    the user has left home or work, and over days the times of those changes show the user's routine.
* **A limited data plan.** A user who marks their home network as metered, or turns on Low Data Mode, may
    have a capped or expensive plan, which can suggest their income.

The feature doesn't reveal the network's type, speed, name, or carrier, or the user's IP address.

<h3 id=privacy-existing>What is already exposed</h3>

Servers see the user's IP address, and IP address ranges usually reveal whether a request came from a
cellular carrier or a fixed-line provider. In most contexts, therefore, the value is close to what the server
can already work out. The Network Information API [[NETINFO]] reveals the connection type to script in the
browsers that support it; Firefox and Safari don't.

This changes when the user agent hides the IP address, as a proxy, relay, or anonymity network does. Then
the feature would reveal what the IP address no longer does, and a change in its value would link visits that
the hidden address no longer links. Those contexts are [=reduced-exposure contexts=], where the feature is
always ''connection/unmetered''.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

The value changes as the user moves, so it is a poor stable identifier. At a moment when a fraction |p| of
users are on metered connections, a user on one reveals −log<sub>2</sub>(|p|) bits. On phones, |p| is large,
because cellular data is common. On desktop computers it is small, so a desktop user on a metered connection
reveals more.

Mitigations:

* Two values only.
* ''connection/unmetered'' in [=reduced-exposure contexts=], including when the IP address is hidden.
* The user can turn the feature off.

<h3 id=privacy-correlation>Linking visits by when the value changes</h3>

When the user walks out of range of their Wi-Fi, every open document could learn that the connection became
metered at the same moment, and a tracker present in two of them could link them. The feature uses
[=delayed change delivery=]:

* Hidden documents learn of the change only when they become visible, which reveals no timing.
* Visible documents learn of it after a random delay of up to 60 seconds, chosen for each document.
* A document learns of at most one change every 10 minutes, so a user at the edge of Wi-Fi range, whose
    connection keeps switching, reveals few moments.

When the network changes, the IP address usually changes too, and a tracker can link the two documents by
their new, shared address when they next make requests. The delay matters most when that doesn't work: when
the IP address is hidden. Those are reduced-exposure contexts, so there the feature reveals no change at all.

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Reading the feature without script</h3>

A style sheet can report the value to a server by loading a resource under the query. The request itself
comes from the user's IP address, which usually reveals the same thing. Documents where script is disabled
for privacy reasons are reduced-exposure contexts.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

Both features have the same value in cross-origin frames as in the top-level document. Embedded video and
advertisements download the most data, and a frame already receives the user's IP address with every request,
and <code>navigator.onLine</code> in its script.

<h3 id=privacy-inference>Sensitive inferences</h3>

A page could treat ''connection/metered'' as a sign that the user is away from home, or can't afford much
data, and change its offers or prices. The value alone is weak evidence of either, because many users on
metered connections are simply using their phones. Users who are concerned can turn the feature off.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* A tracker that sees the same user's documents visible during several network changes, while it can't use
    the IP address, can still link them.
* On desktop computers, where metered connections are uncommon, ''connection/metered'' reveals more.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* Neither feature changes the network or reveals anything about it that a request doesn't.
* Pages must not use '@media/connection' for billing, or to decide whether a user can afford something. The
    operating system can be wrong: many hotspots aren't marked as metered.
* A page could use more of the user's bandwidth when the connection is unmetered, for example by sharing
    uploads between users. Pages can already do this. The feature doesn't give a page permission to use the
    user's connection for anyone else.
* An offline message in a page is part of the page. It isn't a message from the user agent, and users
    shouldn't be asked to enter credentials because of it.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-type>Connection type and speed</h3>

Features like <code>(connection-type: cellular)</code> and <code>(downlink < 1mbps)</code> would give
pages more to work with. They were not chosen because the type reveals more about where the user is than
whether they pay for data, and speeds change often enough to link visits by timing. A page that knows the
connection is metered knows what it needs to.

<h3 id=alt-reduced-data>Folding metered connections into '@media/prefers-reduced-data'</h3>

User agents could match '@media/prefers-reduced-data' on metered connections. That would give no new
information to pages, but the feature is defined as the user's preference, and a user on a metered
connection may still want full-quality media. Authors can combine the two, as the example in the
introduction does.

<h3 id=alt-network-name>''(online)'' as its own feature</h3>

A feature named <code>online</code> would read naturally in a boolean context. '@media/network' was chosen
so the offline state has a name of its own, and <code>(network)</code> already means online.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should ''connection/metered'' be split into ''connection/metered'' and a value for Low Data
Mode, which is closer to a preference?

<p class=issue>Should '@media/network' have a value for "captive portal", which operating systems can
detect and pages often break under? It would reveal something <code>navigator.onLine</code> doesn't.


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Whether the user agent is online, for offline interfaces, and whether the network is metered, so pages
    can download less.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. '@media/network' exposes no more than <code>navigator.onLine</code>. '@media/connection' exposes
    one bit and no network type, speed, or name.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: Changes to '@media/connection' observed over time can suggest the user's routine. See
    [[#privacy-exposed]].
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: A metered connection can suggest a limited data plan. See [[#privacy-inference]].
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Yes: on phones, whether the user is on cellular data, and when they leave home or work.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Yes: the operating system's report of whether the network is metered.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: The moment '@media/connection' changes. Delayed change delivery limits this, and contexts where the IP
    address is hidden get no changes. See [[#privacy-correlation]].
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: It doesn't. See [[#privacy-third-party]].
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: In a mode that protects against fingerprinting or hides the IP address, '@media/connection' is
    ''connection/unmetered''. '@media/network' is unchanged, like <code>navigator.onLine</code>.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: '@media/network' is updated when the document is restored, as <code>navigator.onLine</code> is.
    '@media/connection' is delivered when it becomes visible.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a value becomes more revealing when other protections, such as hiding the IP address, are on.
